<tbody><tr><th style="width: 30%; background: #ACE1AF; text-align: center;" colspan="2">Zincke reaction</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>Named after</td>
<td><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Theodor_Zincke" title="Theodor Zincke">Theodor Zincke</a></td></tr>
<tr>
<td>Reaction type</td>
<td><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Coupling_reaction" title="Coupling reaction">Coupling reaction</a></td></tr>
<tr>









<td style="width: 30%; background: #ACE1AF; text-align: center;" colspan="2"></td></tr>

</tbody></table>
<p id="mwBA">The <b id="mwBQ">Zincke reaction</b> is an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Organic_reaction" title="Organic reaction" id="mwBg">organic reaction</a>, named after <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Theodor_Zincke" title="Theodor Zincke" id="mwBw">Theodor Zincke</a>, in which a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Pyridine" title="Pyridine" id="mwCA">pyridine</a> is transformed into a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Pyridinium_salt" title="Pyridinium salt" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCQ">pyridinium salt</a> by reaction with 2,4-dinitro-chlorobenzene and a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Primary_amine" title="Primary amine" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCg">primary amine</a>.<sup about="#mwt6" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-1"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-1" id="mwCw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQ">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt9" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-2"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-2" id="mwDw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEQ">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt12" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-3" id="mwEw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFQ">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt15" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Zincke338_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Zincke338"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Zincke338-4"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-Zincke338-4" id="mwFw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwGA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGQ">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-halign-center" typeof="mw:File" id="mwGw"><a href="./File:Zincke_Reaction.svg" class="mw-file-description" title="The Zincke reaction" id="mwHA"><img resource="./File:Zincke_Reaction.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Zincke_Reaction.svg/500px-Zincke_Reaction.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Zincke_Reaction.svg/1280px-Zincke_Reaction.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="The Zincke reaction" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="248" data-file-type="drawing" height="242" width="500" class="mw-file-element" id="mwHQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwHg">The Zincke reaction</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwHw">The Zincke reaction should not be confused with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Zincke-Suhl_reaction" title="Zincke-Suhl reaction" class="mw-redirect" id="mwIA">Zincke-Suhl reaction</a> or the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Zincke_nitration" title="Zincke nitration" id="mwIQ">Zincke nitration</a>. Furthermore, the Zincke reaction has nothing to do with the chemical element <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Zinc" title="Zinc" id="mwIg">zinc</a>.</p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}' id="mwIw"/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwJA"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Reaction_mechanism">Reaction mechanism</h2>
<p id="mwJQ">The first reaction is the formation of the <i id="mwJg">N</i>-2,4-dinitrophenyl-pyridinium salt (<b id="mwJw">2</b>).  This salt is typically isolated and purified by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Recrystallization_(chemistry)" title="Recrystallization (chemistry)" id="mwKA">recrystallization</a>.</p>

<figure class="mw-halign-center" typeof="mw:File" id="mwKQ"><a href="./File:Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism1.png" class="mw-file-description" title="The formation of the DNP-pyridinium salt" id="mwKg"><img resource="./File:Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism1.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism1.png/330px-Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism1.png/960px-Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="The formation of the DNP-pyridinium salt" data-file-width="1039" data-file-height="619" data-file-type="bitmap" height="179" width="300" class="mw-file-element" id="mwKw"/></a><figcaption id="mwLA">The formation of the DNP-pyridinium salt</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwLQ">Upon heating a primary amine with the <i id="mwLg">N</i>-2,4-dinitrophenyl-pyridinium salt (<b id="mwLw">2</b>), the addition of the amine leads to the opening of the pyridinium ring.  A second addition of amine leads to the displacement of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./2,4-dinitroaniline" title="2,4-dinitroaniline" class="mw-redirect" id="mwMA">2,4-dinitroaniline</a> (<b id="mwMQ">5</b>) and formation of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./König_salt?action=edit&amp;redlink=1" title="König salt" class="new" typeof="mw:LocalizedAttrs" data-mw-i18n='{"title":{"lang":"x-page","key":"red-link-title","params":["König salt"]}}' id="mwMg">König salt</a><sup about="#mwt18" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-5"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-5" id="mwMw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwNA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNQ">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNg">]</span></span></a></sup> (<b id="mwNw">6a</b> and <b id="mwOA">6b</b>).  The trans-cis-trans isomer of the König salt (<b id="mwOQ">6a</b>) can react by either <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sigmatropic_reaction" title="Sigmatropic reaction" id="mwOg">sigmatropic rearrangement</a> or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Nucleophilic_addition" title="Nucleophilic addition" id="mwOw">nucleophilic addition</a> of a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Zwitterion" title="Zwitterion" id="mwPA">zwitterionic</a> intermediate to give cyclized intermediate (<b id="mwPQ">7</b>).<sup about="#mwt21" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-6" id="mwPg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQA">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQQ">]</span></span></a></sup>  This has been suggested to be the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Rate-determining_step" title="Rate-determining step" id="mwQg">rate-determining step</a>.<sup about="#mwt24" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-7" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-7"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-7" id="mwQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwRA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRQ">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt27" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-8" id="mwRw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwSA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSQ">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSg">]</span></span></a></sup>  After proton transfer and amine <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Elimination_reaction" title="Elimination reaction" id="mwSw">elimination</a>, the desired pyridinium ion (<b id="mwTA">9</b>) is formed.</p>

<figure class="mw-halign-center" typeof="mw:File" id="mwTQ"><a href="./File:Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism2.png" class="mw-file-description" title="The mechanism of the Zincke reaction" id="mwTg"><img resource="./File:Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism2.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism2.png/960px-Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism2.png/1920px-Zincke_Reaction_Mechanism2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="The mechanism of the Zincke reaction" data-file-width="2693" data-file-height="1498" data-file-type="bitmap" height="362" width="650" class="mw-file-element" id="mwTw"/></a><figcaption id="mwUA">The mechanism of the Zincke reaction</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwUQ">This mechanism can be referred to as an instance of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./ANRORC_mechanism" title="ANRORC mechanism" id="mwUg">ANRORC mechanism</a>: nucleophilic addition (A<sub id="mwUw">N</sub>), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ring-opening_reaction" title="Ring-opening reaction" class="mw-redirect" id="mwVA">ring opening</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ring-closing_reaction" title="Ring-closing reaction" class="mw-redirect" id="mwVQ">ring closing</a>.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwVg"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Applications">Applications</h2>
<p id="mwVw">In one <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Solid-phase_synthesis" title="Solid-phase synthesis" id="mwWA">solid-phase synthesis</a> application, the amine is covalently attached to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wang_resin?action=edit&amp;redlink=1" title="Wang resin" class="new" typeof="mw:LocalizedAttrs" data-mw-i18n='{"title":{"lang":"x-page","key":"red-link-title","params":["Wang resin"]}}' id="mwWQ">Wang resin</a>.<sup about="#mwt30" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-9" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-9"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-9" id="mwWg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwWw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwXA">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwXQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-halign-center" typeof="mw:File" id="mwXg"><a href="./File:ZinckeReactionSolidState.png" class="mw-file-description" title="The Zincke reaction" id="mwXw"><img resource="./File:ZinckeReactionSolidState.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/ZinckeReactionSolidState.png/500px-ZinckeReactionSolidState.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/ZinckeReactionSolidState.png/960px-ZinckeReactionSolidState.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="The Zincke reaction" data-file-width="1070" data-file-height="759" data-file-type="bitmap" height="284" width="400" class="mw-file-element" id="mwYA"/></a><figcaption id="mwYQ">The Zincke reaction</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwYg">Another example is the synthesis of a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Chirality_(chemistry)" title="Chirality (chemistry)" id="mwYw">chiral</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Isoquinoline" title="Isoquinoline" id="mwZA">isoquinolinium</a> salt.<sup about="#mwt33" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-10" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-10"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-10" id="mwZQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwZg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZw">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwaA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-halign-center" typeof="mw:File" id="mwaQ"><a href="./File:Zinckereactionchiral.png" class="mw-file-description" title="The Zincke reaction" id="mwag"><img resource="./File:Zinckereactionchiral.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Zinckereactionchiral.png/500px-Zinckereactionchiral.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Zinckereactionchiral.png/960px-Zinckereactionchiral.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="The Zincke reaction" data-file-width="1039" data-file-height="355" data-file-type="bitmap" height="137" width="400" class="mw-file-element" id="mwaw"/></a><figcaption id="mwbA">The Zincke reaction</figcaption></figure>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwbQ"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Zincke_aldehydes">Zincke aldehydes</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt34" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zincke aldehyde"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwbg"><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1368532237" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt35" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'>.mw-parser-output .hatnote{font-style:italic}.mw-parser-output div.hatnote{padding-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.5em}.mw-parser-output .hatnote i{font-style:normal}.mw-parser-output .hatnote+.mw-empty-elt+.hatnote{margin-top:-0.5em}@media print{body.ns-0 .mw-parser-output .hatnote{display:none!important}}</style></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt34" id="mwbw">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Zincke_aldehyde" title="Zincke aldehyde">Zincke aldehyde</a></div>
<p id="mwcA">With secondary amines and not primary amines the Zincke reaction takes on a different shape forming so-called <b id="mwcQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Zincke_aldehyde" title="Zincke aldehyde" id="mwcg">Zincke aldehydes</a></b> in which the pyridine ring is ring-opened with the terminal <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Iminium" title="Iminium" id="mwcw">iminium</a> group hydrolyzed to an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Aldehyde" title="Aldehyde" id="mwdA">aldehyde</a>:<sup about="#mwt36" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Zincke338_4-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Zincke338"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-Zincke338-4" id="mwdQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwdg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mweA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-halign-center" typeof="mw:File" id="mweQ"><a href="./File:Zincke-Aldehyde.svg" class="mw-file-description" title="Zincke aldehydes" id="mweg"><img resource="./File:Zincke-Aldehyde.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Zincke-Aldehyde.svg/500px-Zincke-Aldehyde.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Zincke-Aldehyde.svg/960px-Zincke-Aldehyde.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Zincke aldehydes" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="114" data-file-type="drawing" height="89" width="400" class="mw-file-element" id="mwew"/></a><figcaption id="mwfA">Zincke aldehydes</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwfQ">This variation has been applied in the synthesis of novel <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Indole" title="Indole" id="mwfg">indoles</a>:<sup about="#mwt39" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-11" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-11" id="mwfw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwgA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgQ">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-halign-center" typeof="mw:File" id="mwgw"><a href="./File:ZinckeAldehydeIndoleApplication.svg" class="mw-file-description" title="Zincke aldehydes Kearney 2006" id="mwhA"><img resource="./File:ZinckeAldehydeIndoleApplication.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/ZinckeAldehydeIndoleApplication.svg/500px-ZinckeAldehydeIndoleApplication.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/ZinckeAldehydeIndoleApplication.svg/960px-ZinckeAldehydeIndoleApplication.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Zincke aldehydes Kearney 2006" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="259" data-file-type="drawing" height="202" width="400" class="mw-file-element" id="mwhQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwhg">Zincke aldehydes Kearney 2006</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwhw">with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Cyanogen_bromide" title="Cyanogen bromide" id="mwiA">cyanogen bromide</a> mediated pyridine activation.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwiQ"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="2007_rediscovery">2007 rediscovery</h2>
<p id="mwig">In 2006 and again in 2007 the Zincke reaction was rediscovered by a research group from Japan <sup about="#mwt45" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-12" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-12"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-12" id="mwiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwjA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjQ">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjg">]</span></span></a></sup> and a group from the USA.<sup about="#mwt48" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-13" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-13"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-13" id="mwjw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkQ">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkg">]</span></span></a></sup> Both groups claimed the synthesis of a 12 membered diaza<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Annulene" title="Annulene" id="mwkw">annulene</a> (structure <b id="mwlA">1</b>) from an N-aryl pyridinium chloride and an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Amine" title="Amine" id="mwlQ">amine</a>, an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Aniline" title="Aniline" id="mwlg">aniline</a> in the case of the Japanese group (depicted below) and an aliphatic amine (anticipating <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Surfactant" title="Surfactant" id="mwlw">surfactant</a> properties) in the case of the American group.</p>

<dl id="mwmA"><dd id="mwmQ"><span typeof="mw:File" data-mw='{"caption":"alleged diaza annulene synthesis Yamaguchi 2007"}' id="mwmg"><a href="./File:DiazaAnnulene.svg" class="mw-file-description" title="alleged diaza annulene synthesis Yamaguchi 2007" id="mwmw"><img resource="./File:DiazaAnnulene.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/DiazaAnnulene.svg/960px-DiazaAnnulene.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/DiazaAnnulene.svg/1280px-DiazaAnnulene.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="alleged diaza annulene synthesis Yamaguchi 2007" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="241" data-file-type="drawing" height="282" width="600" class="mw-file-element" id="mwnA"/></a></span></dd></dl>

<p id="mwnQ">In a letter to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Angewandte_Chemie" title="Angewandte Chemie" id="mwng">Angewandte Chemie</a>, the German chemist Manfred Christl <sup about="#mwt51" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-14" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-14"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-14" id="mwnw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwoA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwoQ">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwog">]</span></span></a></sup> pointed out not only that the alleged new chemistry was in fact 100-year-old Zincke chemistry but also that the proposed structure for the reaction product was not the 12 membered ring but the 6 membered pyridinium salt (structure <b id="mwow">2</b>). Initially both groups conceded that they had ignored existing literature on Zincke but held on to the annulene structure based on their <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Electrospray_ionization" title="Electrospray ionization" id="mwpA">electrospray ionization</a> (ESI) results which according to them clearly showed dimer. In his letter Christl remarked that in ESI measurements association of molecules is a common phenomenon. In addition, he noted similarities in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Melting_point" title="Melting point" id="mwpQ">melting point</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./NMR_spectroscopy" title="NMR spectroscopy" class="mw-redirect" id="mwpg">NMR spectroscopy</a>.</p>

<p id="mwpw">As of December 2007 the Japanese group retracted its paper in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Organic_Letters" title="Organic Letters" id="mwqA">Organic Letters</a> <i id="mwqQ">due to uncertainties regarding what products are formed in the reaction described</i> and the US group added a correction to theirs in the Angewandte Chemie stating <i id="mwqg">they wish(ed) to alter the proposed structure of (the) annulene</i>.<sup about="#mwt54" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-15" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-15"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-15" id="mwqw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwrA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrQ">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrg">]</span></span></a></sup> The issue did receive some media coverage:<sup about="#mwt57" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-16" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-16"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-16" id="mwrw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwsA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsQ">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt60" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-17" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-17"}}'><a href="./Zincke_reaction#cite_note-17" id="mwsw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwtA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtQ">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

